Certainly! Below is a PHP script that uses the `qrlaravel` library to create and serve QR codes for user-provided URLs. The script includes basic command line options for download, sharing on social media, and handling user inputs. It also incorporates error-handling to keep things secure and robust.

First, you'll need to set up the following: 
1. Install the `qrlaravel` library. You can do this with Composer:
   ```bash
   composer require qr-laravel/qr
   ```

2. Set up a new route and a new controller endpoint in your Laravel application for QR code creation. Here's a basic command line for creating and using new Laravel projects - assuming you are in your PHP project directory and using NPM for dependencies:

   ```bash
   npx create-laravel qr-code-generator
   cd qr-code-generator
   php artisan make:controller QRCodeController
   ```

Ensure that in your Laravel project, you configure the qrlaravel service as follows: in your `config/services.php`:

```php
'qrlaravel' => [
    'key' => env('QRLARAVEL_KEY'),
    'secret' => env('QRLARAVEL_SECRET'),
],
```

Then in your `.env`:

```bash
QRLARAVEL_KEY=your_api_key
QRLARAVEL_SECRET=your_api_secret
```

Here is a basic command line to create and serve your QR Code with PHP:

```php
<?php

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route;
use App\Http\Controllers\QRCodeController;

// In your routes file (web.php)
Route::get('/generate-qrcode', [QRCodeController::class, 'generateQrCode']);

// In your controller (QRCodeController.php)
namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Qrlaravel\Qr;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Storage;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Log;

class QRCodeController extends Controller
{
    public function generateQrCode(Request $request)
    {
        // Sanitize the userinput to prevent SQL injection and other types of injection. 
        $inputUrl = filter_var($request->url, FILTER_SANITIZE_URL);

        // Check if the URL is in a format that can be used by the library
        if (!filter_var($inputUrl, FILTER_VALIDATE_URL)) {
            return response()->json(['error' => 'Invalid URL'], 400);
        }

        // Generate the QR code and save to the filesystem
        $qr = new \Qrlaravel\Qr($inputUrl);
        $filename = 'qr_code_' . md5($inputUrl) . '.png';
        $qr->toFile(base_path('public', 'qrcode', $filename));

        // Prepare downloads and sharing data
        $download_url = asset("{$filename}");
        $social_media_url = "http://social-sharing-url.com/{$filename}";

        $response = [
            'qr_url' => $download_url,
            'social_media_url' => $social_media_url,
            'qr_code_filename' => $filename
        ];

        return response()->json($response, 200);
    }
}
```

This script, when used, will take the userinputted `url` from the request, sanitize it, and pass it to the qrlaravel library to create and save to the filesystem. The QR code filename is also used by the social media and download links, which you could replace by their own unique and secure URLs.

For the user experience on your frontend, you could use standard input form in your view to display and submit to the API endpoint:

